---
id: org.dxos.echo-memory
name: EchoMemory
version: 0.1.0
---

`@dxos/echo-memory` defines **memory**: what a space knows, held as attributed
facts, and the subscriptions that wake when new facts arrive. It is the one interface that replaces two
that grew separately and overlap:

- `BrainService` (`@dxos/plugin-agent`): per-agent facts plus goal-rule subscriptions, an outbox per
  subscription, read cursors and the clock.
- `FactStore` (`@dxos/pipeline-rdf`): an RDF fact store with structured and SPARQL queries, an ontology
  of predicates and ingest cursors.

The package holds **interface definitions only**: the `MemoryService` tag and its API, the `RdfStore`
port a memory runs on, the fact data model and the small pure functions over it. It contains no storage,
no SPARQL engine and no rule evaluator, so any package can import it — an operation definition, a UI
component, an EDGE worker — without pulling an implementation into its bundle [F-1]. Like `@dxos/echo`,
it is a flat set of noun modules (`Fact`, `MemoryQuery`, `MemoryService`, `RdfStore`, `Subscription`,
`MemoryError`, and the RDF mapping `Graph`, `Mapping`, `Ontology`, `Predicate`, `Sparql`, `Vocab`, `Hash`),
each one also a subpath of its own, so a consumer that must not load the RDF library imports only the
modules it names [F-1.4]. Operations ask for `MemoryService` (or `RdfStore`) in their `services` like
any other space service [F-1.5].

Implementations live elsewhere. `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite` holds the memory core, `RdfMemory`: a
MemoryService over any `RdfStore`, with the goal-rule evaluator and every record it keeps stored as quads.
Beside it is a local `RdfStore` over `@dxos/sparql-sqlite`'s quad store, which the CLI, tests and node
servers open in one process [F-7]. EDGE serves the same interface from a Durable Object over that same core
[F-7.4].

In the app the memory and the store both run in the client-services backend, one memory per space, so every
page and operation shares its rule evaluation, its outboxes and its clock. The backend runs `@dxos/sparql-sqlite`
over the SQLite database it already holds, and the memory core (`RdfMemory`) over that store. Every shared write
goes first to the space's feed in the `facts` namespace, which is durable and replicates like any feed, and only
then to the store, which remembers how far it has read the feed and can be rebuilt from it. Subscriptions and
outboxes are quads in the store's local part, which is never logged or replicated [F-9]. A store's quads move in and out in bulk as TriG or a compact binary form, serialized
where the store is [F-10].

`BrainService` and `FactStore` are deprecated in favour of `MemoryService`; `docs/MIGRATION.md` lists the
work to get there, both the implementations still missing and the call sites to move [F-8].

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.

| Term       | URI                         |
| ---------- | --------------------------- |
| `type`     | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`     |
| `service`  | `org.dxos.mdl.service@1.0`  |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`     |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0` |
| `rule`     | `org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0`     |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |
| `question` | `org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0` |

## Service

```mdl
service MemoryService
  desc: |
    Memory of one space, in two halves over one store.
    - Knowledge base: `put` stores facts; `query` reads them back, with one MemoryQuery per kind of read.
    - Event base: a subscription carries goal rules; whenever put facts or the clock wake them, an event is
      queued in that subscription's own outbox, which its consumer drains with `take` and `ack`.
    Every op is an Effect that fails only with MemoryError. Context tag `@dxos/echo-memory/MemoryService`.
  ops:
    put(facts: Fact[], options?: PutOptions) → number           # events queued [F-3.1, F-4.3]
    query<T>(query: MemoryQuery<T>) → T                         # every knowledge-base read [F-3.2]
    reset() → void                                              # [F-3.6]
    subscribe(subscription: Subscription) → boolean             # false at MAX_SUBSCRIPTIONS [F-4.1]
    subscriptions(owner?: EntityKey) → Subscription[]           # oldest first [F-4.1]
    unsubscribe(id: SubscriptionId) → boolean                   # [F-4.2]
    take(id: SubscriptionId) → MemoryEvent[]                    # oldest first [F-4.4]
    ack(id: SubscriptionId, events: MemoryEventId[]) → void     # [F-4.4]
  errors:
    MemoryError: the store refused, could not be reached, or the input does not compile; the message says which
  note: |
    An instance is scoped to one space, so no op takes a space or agent argument [F-5].
```

### Operations

**Knowledge base**

- **`put(facts, options?) → number`** writes facts to the space's memory. Writing the same fact again
  changes nothing, so a source can be re-read safely. Predicates the memory has not seen are added to
  its ontology. `options.read` moves the source's read cursor in the same write, so memory never claims
  to have read facts it does not hold; `put([], { read })` moves only the cursor. `options.quiet` lists
  speakers whose facts are stored but wake no subscription on their own, so an agent is not woken by
  its own words. Returns how many events the write queued across all subscriptions [F-3.1, F-4.3].
- **`query(MemoryQuery<T>) → T`** is the only read of the knowledge base. The query's kind fixes what it
  returns: facts matching a pattern, the facts in SPARQL-selected graphs, SPARQL rows, the known
  predicates, or a source's read cursor (see [Queries](#queries)). It never changes the memory
  [F-3.2 to F-3.5].
- **`reset() → void`** forgets what the memory knows: facts, read cursors and queued events, and the
  ontology goes back to the seed vocabulary. Subscriptions stay, with their rule state started over,
  so a watch outlives a reset. Sources are read again from the start because their cursors are gone
  [F-3.6].

**Event base**

A subscription is a standing question: "tell me when these rules hold". Its life is:

1. `subscribe` registers it.
2. Each `put`, and the passing of time, may wake its rules; every wake queues one MemoryEvent in the
   subscription's own outbox.
3. Its consumer calls `take` to read the outbox and `ack` once it has handled the events.
4. `unsubscribe` ends it.

- **`subscribe(subscription) → boolean`** adds a subscription, or replaces the one with the same id.
  It wakes only on facts written from `createdAt` on, though its rules may read earlier facts. Returns
  false, adding nothing, when the space already holds `MAX_SUBSCRIPTIONS` others; fails with MemoryError
  when its rules do not compile [F-4.1].
- **`subscriptions(owner?) → Subscription[]`** lists subscriptions, oldest first: all of them, or only
  those whose `owner` is the given agent [F-4.1, F-5.3].
- **`unsubscribe(id) → boolean`** removes a subscription and drops its outbox, events included. Returns
  false when it was already gone, so when two consumers race to end a one-time subscription, exactly
  one sees true and acts on it [F-4.2].
- **`take(id) → MemoryEvent[]`** reads a subscription's unacknowledged events, oldest first, without
  removing them; an unknown id gives an empty list. Calling it twice returns the same events, so a
  consumer that fails before acknowledging sees them again [F-4.4].
- **`ack(id, events) → void`** removes the named events from the outbox, once their consumer has handled
  them. Ids it does not hold are ignored, so acknowledging twice is safe. An acknowledged event is not
  queued again even if the same wake is derived later [F-4.4].

### RdfStore

```mdl
service RdfStore
  desc: |
    A space's quads in two parts: a shared part, replicated, every write to which is recorded durably before it
    is applied; and a local part, the device's own. A memory runs over this port wherever the store is: in the
    same process, or across the client-services bridge. Every op fails only with RdfStoreError. Context tag
    `@dxos/echo-memory/RdfStore`.
  ops:
    query(sparql: string, options?: { local?: boolean }) → Effect<SparqlRow[], RdfStoreError>
    write(writes: Write[], options?: { local?: boolean }) → Effect<string, RdfStoreError>
    export(format: RdfFormat) → Effect<Uint8Array, RdfStoreError>
    import(data: Uint8Array, format: RdfFormat) → Effect<string, RdfStoreError>
    listen(onApplied: (batch: Batch) → void) → Effect<void, never, Scope>
```

- **query**: runs a SPARQL read (SELECT, ASK or CONSTRUCT) over the shared part, or the local part with
  `local`. An update fails: changes go through `write`.
- **write**: applies the writes as one batch, in order, and returns the batch id once it is durable (shared)
  or written (local). A `clear` empties only the part it is written to.
- **export**: every quad of the shared part, serialized where the store is, so a large store crosses a
  boundary once [F-10.1].
- **import**: parses the data where the store is and inserts its quads into the shared part as one batch
  [F-10.2].
- **listen**: calls back with each shared batch once applied, this replica's and other replicas', in order;
  in process the listener is registered before the op returns.

```mdl
type Quad
  desc: A triple in the default graph, or a quad in the named graph `g`.
  fields:
    s: SparqlTerm
    p: SparqlTerm
    o: SparqlTerm
    g?: SparqlTerm
```

```mdl
type Write
  desc: |
    One change to a store: `insert` adds quads (one stored already is kept once), `delete` removes them (one not
    stored is ignored), `clear` removes every quad of the part written to. Tagged by `_tag`.
  literals: insert | delete | clear
```

```mdl
type Batch
  desc: Writes recorded together and applied together; the unit the fact feed carries and `listen` reports.
  fields:
    id: string
    writes: Write[]
```

```mdl
type RdfFormat
  desc: |
    `trig`: the Turtle family's text syntax with named graphs, which every fact is stored in and plain Turtle
    cannot express. `binary`: a term dictionary followed by the quads as indexes into it, several times smaller.
  literals: trig | binary
```

## Data model

The fact model is the one `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` defines today, moved here unchanged in shape so stored
facts and their JSON stay valid [F-2.1]. Every type below is an Effect `Schema` with an interface of the
same name.

### Identifiers

Every id in the model is a branded string (Effect `Schema.String.pipe(Schema.brand(...))`): its JSON
form is a plain string, so stored facts still decode [F-2.1], but the type checker keeps one kind of id
from being passed where another is expected [F-2.4].

```mdl
type FactId
  desc: A statement's id, derived from its graph and the statement; stable across writes.
```

```mdl
type GraphIri
  desc: The IRI of a named graph; what `graph`, `supersedes` and `retracts` hold.
```

```mdl
type EntityKey
  desc: |
    An entity: an absolute IRI where one is known (`echo://…`, `did:…`, `mailto:…`, a URL), else a
    normalized slug local to the graph that mentions it. Names speakers, subjects, objects and owners.
```

```mdl
type SourceUri
  desc: The item facts were read from, or a source read into facts: a DXN or another URI.
```

```mdl
type SubscriptionId
  desc: A subscription's id, unique in its space; chosen by the caller.
```

```mdl
type MemoryEventId
  desc: An event's id, stable per subscription, wake and facts, so a wake derived twice queues once.
```

### What a fact records

A fact is not just a triple. It is a triple plus everything needed to decide how far to trust it, and
whether it still holds. Take one sentence from a chat:

> Bob, 2026-11-03: "I think Alice will probably join Acme in January."

That sentence becomes one Fact with these parts:

| Part          | Question it answers                          | For the example                                                     |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `assertion`   | What is claimed?                             | subject `alice`, predicate `joins`, object `acme`, `validFrom: 2026-01` |
| `factuality`  | How committed is the speaker to it?          | `PR+`: probable and positive ("I think … probably")                 |
| `illocution`  | What was the speaker doing by saying it?     | absent, so `assertive`: Bob informs; he does not ask or promise     |
| `attribution` | Who said it, where, and when?                | agent `bob`, source the message's DXN, `generatedAtTime` 2026-11-03 |
| `recordedAt`  | When did memory learn it?                    | the time the extractor stored it, e.g. 2026-11-16                   |
| `extractor`   | Which program and model read it?             | the extractor id, its model and version                             |

The parts in detail:

- **Assertion** is the proposition itself: a subject, a predicate and an object (RDF's triple). A
  subject or object is a Term, which is either an entity (`alice`, with its surface label `Alice` and
  a kind such as `person`) or a literal value (`"January"`). `validFrom` and `validTo` bound when the
  claimed state is true in the world, and `quote` keeps the words it was read from.
- **Factuality** is the speaker's epistemic commitment, from FactBank. `value` combines a degree —
  `CT` certain, `PR` probable, `PS` possible — with a polarity: `+` it is so, `-` it is not so.
  `CTu` is certain but of unknown polarity; `Uu` is uncommitted, as in a question ("Is Alice joining
  Acme?") or a request. `confidence` is the extractor's own confidence in its reading, 0..1, not the
  speaker's.
- **Illocution** is the speech act (Searle). `assertive` informs, `directive` asks someone to act or
  answer (`mood` tells a question from a request), `commissive` commits the speaker ("I'll send it
  Friday"), `expressive` states a feeling ("thanks"). Wake rules use it, so a question can wake an
  agent that a statement would not.
- **Attribution** is provenance, named after PROV-O: `agent` is who said it (an entity id, with
  `agentLabel` for display), `source` is the DXN of the item it came from, `generatedAtTime` is when
  it was said, and `span` locates it in that item.
- **Two times.** `attribution.generatedAtTime` is when the statement was made (valid time of the
  utterance); `recordedAt` is when memory stored it (transaction time). A mailbox read a month late
  has old `generatedAtTime`s and new `recordedAt`s; ordering by the first replays the conversation,
  by the second replays what memory learned.
- **Run and change detection.** `extractor` names the program and model, `pass` groups the facts one
  extraction run produced (so a run can be replayed or withdrawn as a whole), and `sourceHash` is the
  hash of the source content read, so a changed source is re-read.

### Graphs and revision

Statements that share all of their provenance — same source, speaker, time, quote, factuality and run
— are stored in one RDF named graph, `graph`, which carries that provenance once. A Fact is one
statement in a graph; its `id` derives from the graph and the statement.

Facts are never edited in place. A later graph changes what holds by naming earlier graphs:
`supersedes` replaces them (Bob later says "Alice joins in March"), `retracts` withdraws them ("Alice
is not joining after all"). `graphStatus` reads every graph as `active`, `superseded` or `retracted`,
and `currentFacts(facts, now)` keeps only the facts of active graphs whose `validTo` has not passed
[F-2.3].

A graph with a `kind` records a free-text memory (a `preference`, a `commitment`) rather than a
fact, and `currentFacts` leaves it out; `origin` says whether the speaker `stated` it or the
agent `inferred` it.

```mdl
type Fact
  desc: |
    One statement and what is known about it. Statements that share their provenance live in one named
    graph (`graph`), which carries that provenance once.
  fields:
    id: FactId                    # the statement's id: derived from the graph and the statement
    graph?: GraphIri              # the named graph holding the statement
    assertion: Assertion
    factuality: Factuality
    illocution?: Illocution       # absent ⇒ assertive
    attribution: Attribution
    recordedAt: string            # ISO transaction time
    extractor: { id: string, model: string, version: string }
    sourceHash: string            # for incremental divergence detection
    pass?: string                 # extraction pass id; groups one run's facts
    kind?: string                 # a free-text memory's sort (`preference`, `commitment`); absent for a fact
    origin?: stated | inferred
    supersedes?: GraphIri[]       # the graphs this one replaces
    retracts?: GraphIri[]         # the graphs this one withdraws
```

```mdl
type Assertion
  fields:
    subject: Term
    predicate: string             # label: the surface form, or the ontology's label once stored
    predicateIri?: string
    predicateDescription?: string # what a minted predicate means, as its extractor defined it
    object: Term
    validFrom?: string            # ISO
    validTo?: string              # ISO
    quote?: string                # source span text
```

```mdl
type Term
  desc: An entity reference or a literal, tagged by `kind`.
  literals: EntityTerm | LiteralTerm
```

```mdl
type EntityTerm
  fields:
    kind: entity
    entity: EntityKey
    label?: string                # surface form, for display
    type?: EntityKind
```

```mdl
type LiteralTerm
  fields:
    kind: literal
    literal: string
```

```mdl
type EntityKind
  literals: person | org | place | event | concept | thing
```

```mdl
type Entity
  fields:
    id: string
    kind: EntityKind
    label: string
    aliases: string[]
    ref?: string                  # DXN of a canonical ECHO object, once resolved
```

```mdl
type Attribution
  desc: PROV-O-named provenance.
  fields:
    agent?: EntityKey             # who said it
    agentLabel?: string
    source: SourceUri             # the item it came from
    generatedAtTime: string       # ISO
    wasDerivedFrom?: string[]
    span?: { start: number, end: number }
```

```mdl
type Factuality
  desc: The author's epistemic commitment to the proposition (FactBank).
  fields:
    value: CT+ | CT- | PR+ | PR- | PS+ | PS- | CTu | Uu
    polarity: + | - | ?
    confidence?: number           # 0..1
    nature?: epistemic | aleatory
```

```mdl
type Illocution
  desc: The speech act the source performed.
  fields:
    force: assertive | directive | commissive | expressive
    mood?: declarative | interrogative | imperative
    addressee?: string
```

```mdl
type GraphStatus
  desc: Whether a named graph still holds.
  literals: active | superseded | retracted
```

```mdl
type FactQuery
  desc: What a FactsQuery selects; every field set must hold.
  fields:
    subjectEntity?: EntityKey     # in the subject position
    entity?: EntityKey            # in the subject or object position
    predicate?: string            # IRI, or a surface form resolved through the memory's ontology
    source?: SourceUri            # the item the facts were read from
    minConfidence?: number        # inclusive lower bound on factuality confidence
```

```mdl
type SparqlTerm
  literals: NamedNode | BlankNode | Literal
  note: |
    Plain data: { termType, value, datatype?, language? }, so rows cross a wire as JSON.
```

```mdl
type SparqlRow
  desc: One SPARQL solution: each bound variable (named without `?`) to its SparqlTerm; unbound ones are absent.
```

```mdl
type PredicateEntry
  desc: A predicate a memory knows, as its ontology graph describes it (RDFS / OWL / SKOS).
  fields:
    iri: string
    name: string                  # lowercase shorthand rules use, e.g. works_on
    label: string
    synonyms?: string[]
    description?: string
    domain?: string
    range?: string
    inverse?: string
    equivalents?: string[]
    definedBy?: string
    created?: string
    creator?: string
    example?: string
    replacedBy?: string
```

```mdl
type ReadCursor
  desc: How far a source has been read into facts.
  fields:
    source: SourceUri             # the source read (a chat, a feed, a mailbox)
    through: string               # URI or hash of the last item read; the next read starts after it
```

```mdl
type PutOptions
  fields:
    quiet?: EntityKey[]           # speakers whose facts are stored but wake nothing on their own
    read?: ReadCursor             # moved in the same write as the facts
```

```mdl
type Subscription
  desc: Goal rules held against a space's memory, and the caller's own record of why.
  fields:
    id: SubscriptionId
    owner?: EntityKey             # the agent that holds it; `subscriptions(owner)` filters by it
    rules: string                 # Datalog goal rules (`@dxos/brain`)
    createdAt: string             # ISO; facts put before it wake nothing
    payload?: Json                # stored and returned as is; never interpreted by memory
```

```mdl
type MemoryEvent
  desc: One wake of a subscription's rules, queued in its outbox until acknowledged.
  fields:
    id: MemoryEventId             # stable per subscription, wake and facts
    subscription: SubscriptionId
    label: string                 # the rules' wake label, `achieved`, or a sub-goal id
    facts: Fact[]                 # empty for a wake the clock caused
    at: string                    # ISO
```

## Queries

A `MemoryQuery<T>` is one read of the knowledge base whose answer has type `T`. It is a tagged struct:
`_tag` names the kind, the other fields are its parameters, and `T` is fixed by the kind. Each kind
has a constructor that returns the typed query (`MemoryQuery.facts(pattern)` is a
`MemoryQuery<Fact[]>`), so `query(MemoryQuery.facts(pattern))` is typed `Fact[]` at the call site
[F-3.2]. A query is plain JSON, and each kind names the schema of its result, so a remote client
decodes an answer by the query's `_tag` [F-3.7].

```mdl
type MemoryQuery
  desc: One read of the knowledge base; the kind fixes the result type T.
  literals: FactsQuery | GraphsQuery | SparqlQuery | PredicatesQuery | CursorQuery
```

```mdl
type FactsQuery
  desc: |
    Facts whose statement matches a pattern. Result: Fact[]. Answered without SPARQL on every
    implementation [F-3.2].
  fields:
    _tag: facts
    pattern: FactQuery
```

```mdl
type GraphsQuery
  desc: |
    Every fact in the named graphs a SPARQL `SELECT ?g` binds. Result: Fact[] [F-3.3].
  fields:
    _tag: graphs
    sparql: string
```

```mdl
type SparqlQuery
  desc: Any SPARQL SELECT, its solutions returned as written. Result: SparqlRow[] [F-3.3].
  fields:
    _tag: sparql
    sparql: string
```

```mdl
type PredicatesQuery
  desc: The predicates the memory knows. Result: PredicateEntry[] [F-3.4].
  fields:
    _tag: predicates
```

```mdl
type CursorQuery
  desc: How far a source has been read. Result: string | undefined, the cursor's `through` [F-3.5].
  fields:
    _tag: cursor
    source: SourceUri
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Interface package

  req F-1.1:
    when: a package imports `@dxos/echo-memory`
    then: it gets the MemoryService tag, its API type, every type in the data model, MemoryError and the
          pure functions of [F-2.3] — and nothing that stores, queries or evaluates

  req F-1.2:
    when: `@dxos/echo-memory` is built
    then: its only runtime dependencies are `effect` and `@dxos/*` packages with no storage of their own
          (`@dxos/errors`, `@dxos/util`); it depends on no RDF, SPARQL, SQL or Datalog library

  req F-1.3:
    when: an operation definition names MemoryService in its `services`
    then: importing that definition loads no implementation chunk

  req F-1.4:
    when: a package imports `@dxos/echo-memory/<Module>` for one of its noun modules
    then: it loads that module and the modules it imports, and nothing else; only the RDF mapping modules
          (`Graph`, `Mapping`, `Ontology`, `Predicate`, `Sparql`, `Vocab`) import the RDF library `n3`, so the
          protocol and client packages, which the workerd bundle reaches, import `RdfStore` and `MemoryQuery`
          by subpath

  req F-1.5:
    when: an operation lists `MemoryService.MemoryService` or `RdfStore.RdfStore` in its `services` and runs
          for a space in the app
    then: the runtime provides the space's memory (over `client.rdf(space)`) or store, as it provides
          `Database.Service`; the memory core loads on the first such request
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Fact data model

  req F-2.1:
    when: a fact written by `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` before the move is decoded with `Fact`
    then: it decodes unchanged; the move renames no field and narrows no type

  req F-2.2:
    when: a type of the data model crosses a wire (an EDGE RPC, an HTTP handler, a host-service proxy)
    then: its encoded form is plain JSON, and decoding it gives back an equal value

  req F-2.3:
    when: code needs to read facts without a store
    then: echo-memory provides the pure functions `graphStatus(facts)`, `currentFacts(facts, now)`,
          `termValue(term)`, `termText(term)` and `factText(fact)`, with the behaviour they have today in
          `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` and `BrainService`

  req F-2.4:
    when: code passes an id
    then: it is typed by its brand (FactId, GraphIri, EntityKey, SourceUri, SubscriptionId,
          MemoryEventId); passing one kind where another is expected is a type error, while the encoded
          form stays a plain string
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Knowledge base

  req F-3.1:
    when: `put` is called with facts
    then: each fact is stored once (a repeated put is idempotent), predicates the memory does not know are
          added to its ontology, the read cursor in `options.read` moves in the same write, and the result
          counts the events queued [F-4.3]; a put with no facts only moves the cursor

  req F-3.2:
    when: `query` is called with a MemoryQuery<T>
    then: it answers with a T for that query's kind; there is no other read op on the knowledge base. A
          FactsQuery returns every stored fact whose statement matches all of the pattern's set fields,
          and an entity key matches both a graph-local slug and its absolute IRI

  req F-3.3:
    when: `query` is called with a GraphsQuery or a SparqlQuery
    then: a GraphsQuery runs a `SELECT ?g` and returns every fact in the graphs it binds; a SparqlQuery
          returns the solutions as SparqlRows; anything but a SELECT fails with MemoryError

  req F-3.4:
    when: `query` is called with a PredicatesQuery
    then: it returns the seed vocabulary every memory starts with, then the predicates its facts minted

  req F-3.5:
    when: `query` is called with a CursorQuery for a source
    then: it returns the `through` of the last ReadCursor put for that source, or undefined; a memory that
          loses its facts loses its cursors with them, so the source is read again

  req F-3.6:
    when: `reset` is called
    then: the memory's facts, cursors and queued events are removed and the seed vocabulary is restored;
          its subscriptions stay, with their rule state started over

  req F-3.7:
    when: a MemoryQuery crosses a wire
    then: it is sent as JSON, and the answer is decoded with the result schema its `_tag` names; a kind
          the implementation does not support fails with MemoryError
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Event base

  req F-4.1:
    when: `subscribe` is called
    then: the subscription is added, or replaces the one with its id; it returns false when the memory
          already holds MAX_SUBSCRIPTIONS (256) others, and fails with MemoryError when its rules do not compile

  req F-4.2:
    when: `unsubscribe` is called
    then: the subscription and its outbox are removed; it returns false when it was already gone, so only
          one caller acts on a one-time subscription

  req F-4.3:
    when: facts are put
    then: every subscription of that memory whose rules wake queues one MemoryEvent per wake; facts whose
          speaker is in `options.quiet` are stored and evaluated but wake nothing on their own

  req F-4.4:
    when: a consumer drains an outbox
    then: `take` returns the unacknowledged events oldest first, and they stay until `ack` names them; an
          event with an id already queued for that subscription is not queued again

  req F-4.5:
    when: a time-driven rule (`elapsed`, `every`, `due`) comes due
    then: the implementation re-evaluates it and queues its wakes by itself; the interface has no op for
          the clock, and a caller only sees the events in the outbox

  req F-4.6:
    when: rules are evaluated by any implementation
    then: they run on `@dxos/brain`'s Evaluator, so a subscription wakes the same way wherever memory runs
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Memory scope

  req F-5.1:
    when: a host needs memory
    then: it gets a MemoryService scoped to one space, as it gets the space's Database.Service; each space
          has exactly one memory store

  req F-5.2:
    when: two spaces' MemoryServices are used
    then: they share no facts, cursors, ontology entries or subscriptions

  req F-5.3:
    when: several agents work in one space
    then: they read and write the space's one store; a subscription's `owner` says which agent holds it,
          and a fact's `attribution.agent` says who said it
```

```mdl
feat F-6: Errors

  req F-6.1:
    when: any op fails
    then: it fails with MemoryError (a `BaseError` from `@dxos/errors`), which replaces both
          `BrainError` and `SemanticIndexError`; implementation errors are wrapped with the cause kept
```

```mdl
feat F-7: SQLite implementation (@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite)

  req F-7.1:
    when: `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite` is used
    then: it provides MemoryService over the `@dxos/sparql-sqlite` quad store, with
          `layer(storage: SqlStorage)`, `layerFile(path)` (node) and `layerMemory` (a private in-memory
          SQLite), each a `Layer<MemoryService>`

  req F-7.2:
    when: subscriptions, outboxes, queued-event ids and read cursors are written
    then: they are rows in the same SQLite database as the facts, so a memory reopened from a file has
          its event base back

  req F-7.3:
    when: memory must be reached from another process (a dev fact server, a CLI against a running node)
    then: echo-memory-sqlite provides an HTTP client layer and a fetch-style handler for MemoryService

  req F-7.4:
    when: EDGE serves a space's memory
    then: one Durable Object per space builds MemoryService with `layer(ctx.storage.sql)` from echo-memory-sqlite,
          so the client and EDGE run one implementation

  req F-7.5:
    when: a SQLite memory is open
    then: it schedules its own re-evaluation of time-driven rules in the layer's scope; a memory opened
          from a file after rules came due while it was closed evaluates them on open
```

```mdl
feat F-8: Deprecation of BrainService and FactStore

  req F-8.1:
    when: echo-memory is published
    then: `BrainService` and `FactStore` (and `FactStoreLive`, `FactStoreHttp`) carry `@deprecated` JSDoc
          naming their MemoryService replacement

  req F-8.2:
    when: every call site in `docs/MIGRATION.md` is moved
    then: `BrainService`, `BrainMemory`, `FactStore`, `FactStoreLive` and `FactStoreHttp` are deleted, with
          no re-export left behind

  req F-8.3:
    when: an agent's turn must be started from a wake
    then: that is not memory's job: `wake` leaves the interface and stays with the agent runtime in
          `@dxos/plugin-agent`
```

```mdl
feat F-9: The app's memory: the store and the memory in the backend, logged to a fact feed

  req F-9.1:
    when: a page or an operation in the app uses MemoryService
    then: its calls cross the client-services bridge as `MemoryService` RPCs to the memory the backend runs for the
          space, beside the space's store: one rule evaluator, one outbox per subscription and one clock per space,
          however many pages and operations call it; `client.memory(spaceId)` returns it

  req F-9.2:
    when: a write to the shared part is made (`put` or `reset` of a memory)
    then: its batch is appended to the space's fact feed, in the `facts` feed namespace, before the store
          changes; the store applies it by reading the feed back

  req F-9.3:
    when: the store applies feed batches
    then: it records the feed cursor it has read through in the same transaction as each batch, and on open
          applies only the batches after that cursor

  req F-9.4:
    when: the store is empty or lost and the feed is not
    then: opening the store replays the whole feed and a memory over it answers every query as before;
          clears in the feed are replayed in order

  req F-9.5:
    when: another replica appends to the fact feed
    then: the store applies the new batches as they arrive, and a memory over it pushes the facts they add to
          its rules, so they wake its subscriptions as a local `put` would

  req F-9.6:
    when: subscriptions, outboxes, acknowledgements or the time of the last evaluation change
    then: they are written to the store's local part as quads, which is never written to the fact feed and is
          untouched by a `clear` of the shared part

  req F-9.7:
    when: the backend reaches its database only through an async connection (Effect's `SqlClient`)
    then: it runs `@dxos/sparql-sqlite`'s async engine over that connection; the sync engine stays for hosts
          with synchronous SQLite (Durable Objects, node, the CLI), and both give the same answers

  req F-9.8:
    when: the backend holds stores for several spaces in its one database
    then: each space's tables carry a prefix of its own, so spaces share no rows and no name meets one of the
          host's tables

  req F-9.9:
    when: a page calls a space's store
    then: `client.rdf(spaceId)` returns an RdfStore whose calls cross the bridge as the `RdfStoreService`
          RPCs; the backend opens that space's store on its first call and closes it with the host
```

```mdl
feat F-10: Moving a store in bulk

  req F-10.1:
    when: a store is exported
    then: every quad of its shared part is serialized beside the store, as TriG or as the binary form, and
          only the result crosses to the caller

  req F-10.2:
    when: data is imported into a store
    then: it is parsed beside the store and its quads are inserted into the shared part as one batch, so the
          import is logged and replicated like any write

  req F-10.3:
    when: a store's export is imported into an empty store, in either format
    then: the second store holds the same quads, blank nodes included, and a memory over it answers the
          same facts
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: Facts round-trip
  given: two MemoryServices from `layerMemory`, for spaces A and B
  when: three facts are put in A and read with a FactsQuery on `entity`
  then:
    - query in A returns the facts whose subject or object is the entity
    - the same query in B returns nothing
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: A subscription wakes and is drained
  given: a space's memory with a subscription owned by `alice` whose rules match `works_on(alice, X)`
  when: a fact `alice works_on dxos` is put
  then:
    - put returns 1
    - take returns one MemoryEvent carrying the fact
    - after ack, take returns nothing
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Quiet speakers wake nothing
  given: the subscription of T-2
  when: the same fact is put with `quiet: [alice]` and alice as the speaker
  then:
    - put returns 0
    - a FactsQuery returns the fact
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: Event base survives a reopen
  given: a memory from `layerFile(path)` with a subscription and one unacknowledged event
  when: the layer is closed and built again from the same path
  then:
    - subscriptions returns the subscription
    - take returns the event
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: Reset keeps subscriptions
  given: a memory with facts, a cursor, a subscription and a queued event
  when: reset is called
  then:
    - a FactsQuery and a CursorQuery return nothing
    - a PredicatesQuery returns only the seed vocabulary
    - subscriptions still returns the subscription; take returns nothing
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: Wire form is JSON
  given: a Fact, a Subscription with a payload, and a MemoryEvent
  when: each is encoded, passed through JSON.stringify and JSON.parse, and decoded
  then: each decodes to a value equal to the original
```

```mdl
scenario T-7: One query op, typed per kind
  given: a memory holding facts about `dxos`
  when: query is called with MemoryQuery.facts, MemoryQuery.sparql and MemoryQuery.cursor
  then:
    - each call is typed at the call site as Fact[], SparqlRow[] and string | undefined
    - each answer, sent through the HTTP handler and client, decodes to an equal value
```

```mdl
scenario T-8: The store is rebuilt from the fact feed
  given: a memory over an empty database and a fact log, with facts put, a reset, and a read cursor moved
  when: a second memory opens over a new, empty database and the same log
  then:
    - a FactsQuery returns the same facts and a CursorQuery the same cursor
    - a fact put through the first memory afterwards appears in the second without a write to it, and wakes
      the second memory's own subscription
    - the first memory has none of the second's subscriptions
```

```mdl
scenario T-9: A store moves in bulk
  given: a memory holding facts, over a store
  when: the store is exported as TriG and as binary, and each export is imported into a new, empty store
  then:
    - a memory over each new store answers the same facts
    - the binary export is smaller than the TriG one
```

## Rules

```mdl
rule no-implementation-deps: echo-memory imports no storage, SPARQL engine or rule evaluator
  files: src/*.ts
  severity: error
  grep: from '(sparqljs|@dxos/sparql-sqlite|@dxos/sql-sqlite|@dxos/brain|@effect/sql[^']*|node:[^']*)'
  question: Does this file import a SPARQL engine or parser, SQL, Datalog or a node-only module?

  `@dxos/echo-memory` is the interface every layer imports; an implementation import here puts that
  machinery in every bundle that names MemoryService [F-1.2].
```

```mdl
rule no-n3: echo-memory does not import the n3 RDF library
  files: src/*.ts
  severity: error
  grep: from 'n3'
  question: Does a non-test module of echo-memory import n3?

  n3 loads `readable-stream`, which fails to load under workerd's `nodejs_compat`; the memory core runs on EDGE and
  needs only RDF/JS terms (`Rdf`) and a TriG writer (`Trig`), so n3 is a test-only cross-check [D-63].
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: `@dxos/echo-memory`, in the ECHO package group, holds the memory service: the combination of `BrainService` and `FactStore`. Both are deprecated in favour of it.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, F-8]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: `@dxos/echo-memory` holds only interface definitions.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1]
```

```mdl
decision D-3: `@dxos/echo-memory` also holds the data structures for facts and related types.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [Fact, F-2]
```

```mdl
decision D-4: `@dxos/echo-memory-native` holds the local implementations, for the CLI and similar hosts: an RDF store over SQLite.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
```

```mdl
decision D-5: The RDF store over SQLite is `@dxos/sparql-sqlite`'s interned quad store, the one `FactStoreLive` already runs on. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7.1]
  why: No package is named rdf-sqlite; sparql-sqlite is the RDF-over-SQLite store in the repo and already runs in node and in Durable Object SQLite.
  rejected:
    - A new RDF table layout written for echo-memory-native, which would fork the SPARQL compiler.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: One MemoryService serves many memories; every op that reads or writes a memory takes its MemoryId. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, F-5]
  why: BrainService is keyed by agent and FactStore is one instance per space; a key covers both, and EDGE routes a key to a Durable Object without a layer per memory.
  rejected:
    - One MemoryService instance per memory, provided as a Layer, which forces hosts that serve many agents to build a layer per call.
```

```mdl
decision D-7: The fact model moves from `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` with its shape unchanged; `FactQuery` merges BrainService's `FactQuery` and pipeline-rdf's `SemanticQuery` (adding `minConfidence`). Fills in [D-3].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [Fact, FactQuery, F-2.1]
  why: Facts are already stored in files, Durable Objects and ECHO feeds; a reshape would need a data migration with no benefit to the interface.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: echo-memory carries no ontology resolution, IRI minting or RDF serialization; those stay with the implementation and the pipeline. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.2, no-implementation-deps]
  why: They depend on n3; an interface package that imports n3 brings it into every UI and operation bundle.
  rejected:
    - Moving `Ontology`, `Mapping` and `Vocab` wholesale.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: A Subscription is goal rules plus an opaque JSON payload; the agent `Trigger` type stays in `@dxos/plugin-agent` and is stored as that payload. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [Subscription, F-4.1]
  why: Trigger holds ECHO refs and a notify action that memory never reads; EDGE already stores it as opaque JSON beside its rules.
  rejected:
    - Moving Trigger into echo-memory, which would give the interface a dependency on plugin-agent's Goal type.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: `wake` is not part of MemoryService; starting an agent's turn stays with the agent runtime. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-8.3]
  why: It needs Database.Service and a Chat, which are not memory, and would make the interface depend on the assistant packages.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: One cursor model: a ReadCursor moves with `put`, and `cursor` reads it; FactStore's `setCursor` and BrainService's `readThrough` both map onto these. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3.1, F-3.5]
  why: Moving the cursor in the same write as the facts means a memory that loses its facts never claims to have read their source.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: MemoryError is the only error of the interface, replacing BrainError and SemanticIndexError. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-13: echo-memory-native has one platform-neutral core over `SqlStorage` that persists the event base beside the facts; EDGE uses it, and the HTTP client and handler live in it too. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
  why: Today the client brain keeps subscriptions in memory maps and EDGE re-implements them in its Durable Object; one core removes the second copy and makes a file-backed CLI memory keep its subscriptions.
```

```mdl
question Q-1: echo-memory-native depends on `@dxos/brain` and `@dxos/sparql-sqlite`, which live under the compute packages. Where should they sit relative to ECHO?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
  context: An ECHO package depending on compute packages reverses the usual layering.
  options:
    - Keep: echo-memory-native depends on the compute packages; only the interface is in ECHO.
    - Move: sparql-sqlite and the brain evaluator move under the ECHO package group.
    - Relocate: echo-memory-native lives under the compute packages instead.
```

```mdl
question Q-2: Does the interface keep raw SPARQL (`select`, `rows`), or only the structured `query`?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3.3]
  context: Every implementation today runs on sparql-sqlite, but SPARQL in the interface ties any future implementation to a SPARQL engine. EDGE's BrainObject does not expose them yet.
  options:
    - Keep: both are required ops.
    - Optional: they fail with MemoryError on implementations without SPARQL.
    - Drop: callers that need SPARQL use the native implementation directly.
```

```mdl
question Q-3: Should MemoryService expose change notifications (a stream that fires after `put` and `reset`)?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService]
  context: plugin-brain wraps its FactStore to notify the UI after writes; without a stream every UI host repeats that wrapper.
  options:
    - Add: `changes(memory) → Stream<void>` is part of the interface.
    - Leave: hosts wrap the service as they do today.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: Each space has one memory store, and the MemoryService a caller gets is scoped to a space.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  supersedes: D-6
  affects: [MemoryService, F-5]
```

```mdl
decision D-15: No op takes a memory, space or agent argument; a Subscription carries an optional `owner`, and `subscriptions(owner?)` filters by it. Fills in [D-14].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, Subscription, F-5.3]
  why: plugin-agent lists one agent's triggers; an owner field does that without memory reading the opaque payload.
  rejected:
    - Filtering by the payload in plugin-agent, which loads every subscription in the space to show one agent's.
```

```mdl
question Q-4: With one store per space, can an agent keep facts the other agents in the space do not read?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5.3]
  context: BrainService today gives each agent its own facts; with a store per space, every agent in the space reads every fact.
  options:
    - Shared: all facts in a space are visible to all agents in it.
    - Owned graphs: a graph may name an owning agent, and query reads only shared graphs and the caller's own.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: Memory has a typed query, `MemoryQuery<T>`. Every query option is combined into one op, `query(MemoryQuery<T>) → T`, and the spec describes each kind of query.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, MemoryQuery, F-3.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-17: The query kinds are `facts`, `graphs`, `sparql`, `predicates` and `cursor`, replacing the ops `query`, `select`, `rows`, `predicates` and `cursor`; the event-base reads (`subscriptions`, `take`, `nextDueAt`) stay ops. Fills in [D-16].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryQuery, F-3.2, F-3.3, F-3.4, F-3.5]
  why: Those five are the knowledge-base reads; the event-base reads are tied to subscription ids and outbox state, which the query kinds do not touch.
```

```mdl
decision D-18: A MemoryQuery is a tagged JSON struct whose `_tag` names the schema of its result; a constructor per kind gives the typed `MemoryQuery<T>`, and an unsupported kind fails with MemoryError. Fills in [D-16].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryQuery, F-3.7]
  why: The query crosses EDGE RPC and HTTP, so T must be recoverable from data; a phantom type alone would leave the remote client unable to decode the answer.
  rejected:
    - One op per kind, which is what D-16 replaces.
```

```mdl
question Q-5: Should the event-base reads (`subscriptions`, `take`, `nextDueAt`) also be MemoryQuery kinds?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryQuery, F-4]
  context: D-17 keeps them as ops. As kinds, every read of memory goes through one op; as ops, `query` stays about facts.
  options:
    - Ops: only knowledge-base reads are query kinds.
    - Kinds: `subscriptions`, `events` and `nextDueAt` join MemoryQuery.
```

```mdl
decision D-19: When time-driven rules are evaluated is a detail of each implementation, not part of the interface; `tick` and `nextDueAt` are removed.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, F-4.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-20: A native memory schedules its own time-driven evaluation in its layer's scope and catches up on open; EDGE drives the same evaluation from its Durable Object alarm. Fills in [D-19].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7.5, F-7.4]
  why: A CLI memory backed by a file is not running while rules come due, so without catch-up a reminder due overnight would never wake.
```

```mdl
decision D-21: Ids in the memory interface are branded types.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [MemoryService, F-2.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-22: The branded ids are FactId, GraphIri, EntityKey, SourceUri, SubscriptionId and MemoryEventId, each a branded string whose JSON form is unchanged. Fills in [D-21].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [FactId, GraphIri, EntityKey, SourceUri, SubscriptionId, MemoryEventId, F-2.4]
  why: These are the ids the ops take or the model cross-links; a brand on the type alone keeps stored facts and wire JSON decoding as before (D-7).
  rejected:
    - Reusing `@dxos/keys` DXN for SourceUri, which would reject the non-DXN URIs read cursors already hold.
    - Branding predicate IRIs, which callers pass as surface forms as often as IRIs.
```

```mdl
decision D-23: The SQLite implementation package is `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite`, not `@dxos/echo-memory-native`.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7]
```

```mdl
decision D-24: In the app, memory calls, SPARQL queries included, go over the client-services bridge to a backend, and the backend runs `@dxos/sparql-sqlite` over the SQLite database it already has.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1]
```

```mdl
decision D-25: Every write goes first to a new feed in the `facts` namespace, for durability and replication, and then to the store. The store remembers its feed cursor and can be rebuilt from the feed, and that rebuild is tested.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.2, F-9.3, F-9.4, T-8]
```

```mdl
decision D-26: The fact feed records `put` (with its facts, read cursor and quiet speakers) and `reset` as entries; subscriptions, outboxes and acknowledgements stay local. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.2, F-9.6]
  why: Replaying puts and resets in order is enough to rebuild the knowledge base; an outbox is one consumer's progress, so replicating it would drain a subscription for every device at once.
```

```mdl
decision D-27: A memory applies entries other replicas append as they arrive, and they wake its own subscriptions as a local put would; it starts listening to the feed before its first read, so no entry lands unseen between them. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.5]
  why: A fact said on another device is new to this device's watches; without the listen-then-read order, a write made just after open was never applied.
```

```mdl
decision D-28: `facts` is a protocol well-known feed namespace beside `data` and `trace`. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.2]
  why: The feed store and replication only carry well-known namespaces; ECHO's indexer reads only `data` and `trace`, so fact blocks never reach the object index.
```

```mdl
question Q-6: Should subscriptions also be written to the fact feed, so a watch set on one device runs on every device?
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.6]
  context: D-26 keeps them local, so each device evaluates only the watches made on it, and a rebuilt store has none.
  options:
    - Local: subscriptions and outboxes stay per device.
    - Replicated: subscriptions go to the feed; outboxes stay local.
```

```mdl
decision D-29: `@dxos/sparql-sqlite` also runs over an async connection; it has a sync and an async variant.
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.7]
```

```mdl
decision D-30: Both variants share one engine: the store and engine logic yield SQL statements that a sync or an async driver runs, and the async variant compiles from caches and retries once with the store reads it missed. Fills in [D-29].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.7]
  why: Two copies of the store and engine would drift; the compiler's lookups are synchronous callbacks, and fetch-and-retry keeps the compiler unchanged.
  rejected:
    - Running the sync engine against a synchronous handle borrowed from the host's async connection, which needs the SQLite clients to expose their connection.
```

```mdl
decision D-31: Each space's memory lives in the host's database under a table prefix of its own; the store's legacy cleanup runs only for an unprefixed store. Fills in [D-24].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.8]
  why: The store's tables (quads, terms, meta, …) had fixed names, so two spaces in one database would share one store and could meet the host's own tables; the old layout's cleanup drops tables named cursors and entities, which a shared database may own.
  rejected:
    - One SQLite database per space, which is not the database the backend already has.
```

```mdl
decision D-32: The backend serves `MemoryService` RPCs and opens a space's memory on its first call; a space's fact feed id is derived from its space id, so every device addresses the same feed; the page gets a space's memory from `client.memory(spaceId)`. Fills in [D-24].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.9]
  why: The host has no per-space open and close hook, and a random feed id per device would give each device its own fact feed instead of one replicated feed.
```

```mdl
decision D-33: Replicating fact feeds is behind a client-services stack option, off by default, until EDGE accepts the `facts` namespace. Fills in [D-25].
  date: 2026-11-16
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.5]
  why: EDGE's pinned feed store rejects appends to an unknown namespace, and the syncer pulls every listed namespace for every space on every poll, so turning it on first would fail pushes and add an EDGE round trip per space for spaces that never use memory.
```

```mdl
decision D-34: client-services does not depend on `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite`, and `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite` is a standalone implementation over native SQLite that pulls in no other SPARQL store.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1, F-7]
```

```mdl
decision D-35: The backend is thin and the memory runs in the page: client-services only runs `@dxos/sparql-sqlite` over its database and appends to the facts feed; the page's MemoryService turns calls into SPARQL over the bridge, and rule evaluation and outboxes live page-side.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1, F-9.9]
  supersedes: D-32
```

```mdl
decision D-36: The store API has import and export verbs, with TriG as one format and a binary form as the other, and the backend forms the export and parses the import, for efficiency.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [RdfStore, F-10]
```

```mdl
decision D-37: Subscriptions and outboxes live in the RDF store, outside the log, and are never replicated.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.6]
  supersedes: D-26
  answers: Q-6
```

```mdl
decision D-38: The RDF type definitions move from `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` to `@dxos/echo-memory`.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  supersedes: D-8
```

```mdl
decision D-39: The port between a memory and its store is `RdfStore`: SPARQL reads, batches of insert, delete and clear writes, bulk import and export, and a listener for applied shared batches; each op reads or writes a shared part or, with `local`, a local part. Fills in [D-35].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [RdfStore, F-9.1, F-9.6]
  why: A memory in the page needs exactly what the backend can give without knowing about memory, and the local part is where subscriptions and outboxes go without reaching the log.
  rejected:
    - Keeping MemoryService as the RPC, which puts the memory (and the rule evaluator) in the backend.
    - SPARQL UPDATE as the write path, which the log could not record as data without parsing it.
```

```mdl
decision D-40: The mapping between facts and RDF, and between fact queries and SPARQL text, is the subpath `@dxos/echo-memory/rdf`; the main entry stays free of `n3`, and the fact schemas are the branded ones in `Fact` with no second copy. Fills in [D-38].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  why: Protocols and the client import the main entry, and n3 on their static graph breaks the workerd bundle; one fact schema means one place for the brands.
  rejected:
    - Moving the RDF modules into the main entry, which would put n3 into every bundle that names MemoryService.
```

```mdl
decision D-41: Hand-built facts are made through `Fact.make` (and `Fact.makeMany`) from their plain form, and `Graph.assign` takes facts in their plain form, since the ids they end up with are known only there. Fills in [D-22].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2]
  why: The brands make a string literal no longer a FactId, so fixtures and extractors need one way to brand a whole fact.
```

```mdl
decision D-42: The memory core is `RdfMemory` in `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite`, importable on its own subpath so the page loads no SQLite; the CLI opens it over `LocalRdfStore`, the same port over `@dxos/sparql-sqlite` in process. Fills in [D-35].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-7, F-9.1]
  why: The core needs the rule evaluator, which `@dxos/echo-memory` must not import; one core keeps the CLI, the page and EDGE alike.
```

```mdl
decision D-43: The fact feed carries the store's batches (insert, delete and clear of quads), not memory entries; a memory's `put` is one batch holding the facts' quads and its read cursor, and `reset` is a clear plus the seed vocabulary. Fills in [D-35].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.2, F-9.4]
  why: The backend no longer knows about facts, only quads, and a batch replays to the same store on every replica.
```

```mdl
decision D-44: A memory pushes the facts in another replica's batch to its rules without `quiet`, which is not recorded in the batch. Fills in [D-43].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.5]
  why: `quiet` names speakers whose facts wake nothing on the device that wrote them; a batch is quads only, and reading speakers back would need memory knowledge in the log.
  rejected:
    - Adding a memory-specific field to every batch, which the backend would carry without understanding.
```

```mdl
decision D-45: In process, `RdfStore.listen` registers its listener before it returns; across the bridge the change stream starts once the host answers. Fills in [D-39].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [RdfStore]
  why: A forked stream gives no sign that it is subscribed, so a write right after a replica opened went unseen; a registered listener closes that gap where the store is in process.
```

```mdl
decision D-46: Operations can request MemoryService the way they request the other services.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.5]
```

```mdl
decision D-47: In the app, a space-affinity layer gives operations the space's RdfStore from `client.rdf(space)` and a MemoryService over it, contributed beside `Database.Service`; the memory core is imported on the first request. Fills in [D-46].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.5]
  why: A space-affinity layer is how every space service reaches an operation, and the layer's scope is the memory's lifetime; the dynamic import keeps the rule evaluator and n3 out of the boot bundle.
```

```mdl
decision D-48: `@dxos/echo-memory` has the structure of `@dxos/echo`: a flat set of noun modules, the RDF mapping included, with no `rdf` subpath.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  supersedes: D-40
```

```mdl
decision D-49: Every noun module is also a subpath export, `hashText` becomes `Hash.text`, and the packages the workerd bundle reaches (protocols, client, client-services, plugin-client) import `RdfStore`, `MemoryQuery` and `MemoryService` by subpath, so n3 stays off their static graph. Fills in [D-48].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  why: With the RDF mapping in the main entry, importing the barrel would put n3 on those packages' static graph, which broke the workerd bundle before.
```

```mdl
decision D-50: Rule evaluation, outboxes and the clock of a space's memory run in the client-services worker, not in the page.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1]
  supersedes: D-35
```

```mdl
decision D-51: The worker holds one store and one memory per space (`SpaceMemories`) and serves both `RdfStoreService` and `MemoryService` RPCs from them; `client.memory(spaceId)` is the page's MemoryService, and operations get it through the same space layer. client-services imports `@dxos/echo-memory-sqlite`'s `RdfMemory` and `LocalRdfStore` modules, by dynamic import. Fills in [D-50].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.1, F-1.5]
  supersedes: D-34
  why: Running the memory in the worker needs its core there; the concern behind D-34 was a second SPARQL store, which `RdfMemory` no longer brings, and the dynamic import keeps the core out of the worker's boot.
  rejected:
    - Moving the memory core to a package of its own, which adds a package for two modules.
```

```mdl
decision D-52: plugin-brain's fact store registry and plugin-agent's in-memory brain move onto MemoryService in this change.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-8.2]
```

```mdl
decision D-53: plugin-agent's BrainService becomes an adapter over the space's MemoryService: a trigger is a subscription owned by its agent with the trigger as payload, facts and cursors are the space's, `reset` resets the space's memory, `tick` does nothing and `nextDueAt` asks the host back within a minute while the agent has watches. Fills in [D-52].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-8.2]
  why: Every BrainService caller is an operation with a space, so a space layer reaches it, and keeping the BrainService API leaves its forty call sites and EDGE's wire unchanged while the facts move.
  rejected:
    - Rewriting every plugin-agent operation onto MemoryService now, which also moves EDGE's BrainObject and its wire.
```

```mdl
decision D-54: A memory reads time from the clock it was opened with, and `take` first evaluates the time-driven rules that have come due. Fills in [D-19].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
  why: A caller's context (an operation's test clock) must not move the memory's time, and a consumer that drains an outbox expects every wake due by now without depending on when the timer fiber last ran.
```

```mdl
decision D-55: `FactStoreMemory` in `@dxos/pipeline-rdf` adapts both ways: a FactStore over a MemoryService for the pipelines that still take one, and a MemoryService over a FactStore (no event base) for a remote fact server, which a story plugin provides at process affinity so it outranks the client's space memory. Fills in [D-52].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-8.2]
  why: The email, Discord and finance pipelines write through FactStore and run on EDGE too, so they keep that seam while the app's side of it becomes the memory.
```

```mdl
decision D-56: The Discord, finance and System One extraction work merged from main uses the moved fact model, and its `Vocabularies` packs join `@dxos/echo-memory` as a noun module.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
```

```mdl
decision D-57: On EDGE a space's memory is one `MemoryObject` Durable Object in db-service, named by the space id and running the same memory over its own SQLite; existing agent brains start fresh, their old fact and trigger tables left unread.
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
  why: One shared store per space, as in the app, rather than one brain per agent.
```

```mdl
decision D-58: Callers on EDGE (operation-service, plugin bundles) reach the space memory directly through db-service's `MemoryServiceEntrypoint`, and `BrainService` there is plugin-agent's `BrainMemory` over it; compute-service's brain calls for facts and triggers are removed, and the agent's `BrainObject` keeps only wakes, sessions, provisioning and roles until each finds its own home. Fills in [D-57].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
```

```mdl
decision D-59: EDGE's `MemoryObject` is a replica of the space's `facts` feed, so the app and EDGE share one memory per space; the fact-feed indexer, plugin-agent's `AddFactFeed`/`RemoveFactFeed` and its `FactItem` feeds are removed, and producers such as the Discord crawl write with `put` or import TriG. Fills in [D-57].
  date: 2026-11-17
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9, F-1.4]
  why: Facts reach a space's memory one way, through its replicated log, instead of a second feed format and an indexer per agent.
```

```mdl
decision D-60: On EDGE the space's `MemoryObject` drives time-driven rules itself: it sets its alarm to the memory's next due time and, when it fires, asks for the due watches of each subscription owner to run, so the agent's `BrainObject` keeps no location and no polling alarm. Fills in [D-57].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
  why: The memory knows when its rules are next due; a per-agent poll every minute both runs late and runs when nothing is due.
```

```mdl
decision D-61: On EDGE a space's memory is inspected through db-service's memory routes (facts, subscriptions and SPARQL for a space, for admins), which replace compute-service's per-agent brain state and fact routes and `BrainServiceEntrypoint.state`. Fills in [D-57].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9]
  why: What the brain view showed now lives in the space memory, so its reads belong beside the `MemoryObject` rather than to one agent.
```

```mdl
decision D-62: A space's fact feed always replicates, and its format (namespace, id and block encoding) is echo-memory's `FactFeed` module, which the client services and EDGE's `MemoryObject` share; the client-services `replicateFacts` option is removed. Fills in [D-59].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-9.5]
  supersedes: D-33
  why: EDGE now accepts the `facts` namespace and lands with this change, and two replicas can only follow one feed if they derive its id and decode its blocks the same way.
```

```mdl
decision D-63: echo-memory has no n3 dependency: its RDF/JS terms and data factory are its own `Rdf` module, and TriG is written by its own `Trig` module, whose output matches n3's writer; n3 stays only as a test dependency that cross-checks both. echo-memory-sqlite imports echo-memory by per-module subpath. Fills in [D-49].
  date: 2026-11-18
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  why: n3 pulls in `readable-stream`, which fails to load under workerd's `nodejs_compat`, so EDGE's `MemoryObject` could not load; the memory core needs only a term factory.
```

## Appendix: Extension Definitions

```mdl
ext type
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0
  desc: A named data structure with typed fields and optional literals.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    fields?: FieldMap        # name[?]: TypeExpr  (# inline comment)
    literals?: UnionList     # a | b | c
    extends?: TypeRef[]
```

```mdl
ext service
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.service@1.0
  desc: An external or system-level dependency providing named operations.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    loading?: eager | lazy
    ops?: OpMap                 # name(params) → ReturnType
    errors?: ErrorMap
    note?: Prose
```

```mdl
ext feat
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0
  desc: A named feature grouping one or more requirements.
  fields:
    desc?: Prose
    req: RequirementList
  nesting: self
```

```mdl
ext scenario
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0
  desc: An acceptance scenario expressed as given / when / then steps.
  fields:
    given?: Step | Step[]
    when?: Step | Step[]
    then: Step | Step[]
    tags?: TagList
```

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```

```mdl
ext decision
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0
  desc: |
    One decision about the spec, attributed to exactly one actor. The inline prose is the decision
    itself, stated as what is now true ("Bounds are derived, never stored."), not as the edit
    that was made.
  fields:
    date: Date                  # YYYY-MM-DD, the day the decision was made
    by: ActorRef                # user:<handle> | agent:<name> — the decider
    for?: UserRef               # agent entries only: the person the agent acted for
    affects?: RefList           # block ids the decision constrains, e.g. [Scene, F-2.1]
    supersedes?: Identifier     # an earlier decision this one replaces
    why?: Prose                 # the reason; on a user entry, only a reason the person gave
    rejected?: ProseList        # alternatives considered and why they lost
    source?: URI                # PR, review comment, issue, or session where it was made
    answers?: Identifier        # a question this decision answers, e.g. Q-1
  inline-prose: true
```

```mdl
ext question
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0
  desc: |
    A choice the spec needs that nobody has made yet, attributed to the actor that asked it. The
    inline prose is the question. It is answered by a later `decision` whose `answers:` names it.
  fields:
    date: Date                  # YYYY-MM-DD, the day it was asked
    by: ActorRef                # user:<handle> | agent:<name> — who asked
    for?: UserRef               # agent questions only: the person the agent acted for
    affects?: RefList           # block ids the answer will constrain
    context?: Prose             # why it is asked, and what depends on the answer
    options?: ProseList         # suggested answers, each `<title>: <description>`
    source?: URI                # PR, review comment, issue, or session where it was asked
  inline-prose: true
```
